    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content Body -->
  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. Understanding the Core Difference</h2>
    <p>
      Both systems accomplish the identical legal requirement: ensuring that generator power can never backfeed through the utility lines, which would endanger utility line workers attempting to restore the power grid. However, their internal electrical architecture differs fundamentally:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mechanical Generator Interlock Kit:</strong> A precision-machined steel or aluminum sliding plate installed directly onto the front deadfront of your main service panel. The slider creates an absolute physical interference: you cannot turn ON the generator breaker without first sliding the plate down, which mechanically forces the main utility breaker into the OFF position.</li>
      <li><strong>Manual Transfer Switch Subpanel:</strong> A separate auxiliary electrical enclosure mounted adjacent to the main panel. It contains dedicated double-throw toggle switches (Line - Off - Gen) for 6, 8, or 10 specific branch circuits that you permanently intercept and splice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Head-to-Head Comparison Table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Feature</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Mechanical Interlock Kit</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Manual Transfer Switch Subpanel</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Hardware Expense</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">$60 – $150 (Kit + 2-Pole Breaker)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">$350 – $650 (Pre-Wired Enclosure)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Installation Labor &amp; Complexity</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Low (Installs on existing panel face)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">High (Must splice 10+ branch circuits in conduit)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Power Distribution Flexibility</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Unlimited: Energizes entire bus bar</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-red-600 font-bold">Rigid: Limited to pre-selected 6–10 circuits</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Load Management Responsibility</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-amber-700 font-bold">User must manually switch off heavy loads</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Preset circuits prevent accidental panel overload</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Integrated Power Wattage Meters</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">None (Requires clamp meters or separate monitor)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Yes (Analog or digital watt meters built in)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Neutral Switching (Separately Derived)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Solid Neutral (Requires Floating Neutral Gen)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Solid Neutral (Switched Neutral Models Rare/Costly)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. The NEC 408.36(D) Breaker Hold-Down Mandate</h2>
    <p>
      A vital code requirement frequently flagged by municipal electrical inspectors during interlock kit installations is <strong>NEC Section 408.36(D)</strong>:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 text-xs italic text-gray-700 my-4">
      "Plug-in-type overcurrent protection devices (circuit breakers) that are backfed must be secured in place by an additional fastener that requires other than a pull to release the device from the mounting means on the panel."
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Because the backfed generator breaker is energized by an external generator, someone removing the metal panel deadfront could accidentally pull the breaker loose while it is hot, exposing live, ungrounded clips. Every panel manufacturer manufactures an official UL-listed hold-down clamp or screw kit (e.g. Square D HOM4RK2HA or Siemens ECMBR2) that secures the breaker firmly to the panel chassis.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Which System Should You Choose?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Choose a Mechanical Interlock Kit if:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>You want the ability to selectively power ANY circuit in your house (e.g., run the well pump for 15 minutes, turn it off, then run the laundry washing machine, then turn on the master bedroom AC).</li>
      <li>You want the lowest-cost, cleanest installation with minimal wiring disruption inside your main panel.</li>
      <li>You are comfortable manually shedding non-essential breakers before flipping the generator breaker ON.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Choose a Manual Transfer Switch if:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your main panel manufacturer does not produce a certified, UL-listed interlock kit (older obsolete panels).</li>
      <li>You want family members or non-technical occupants to be able to restore power simply by flipping clearly marked toggle switches without touching main panel breakers.</li>
      <li>You want built-in analog wattage meters on the front panel to monitor generator load balance in real time.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

