    note="A dangerous trend online is cheap $25 3D-printed plastic or stamped generic sheet metal interlock sliders sold without UL testing. Electrical inspectors will red-tag and fail these immediately! NEC 110.3(B) states all equipment must be installed and used in accordance with its listing and labeling. Uncertified plastic brackets warp in hot garages, snap under forceful operation, or flex enough to allow both the utility main and the generator breaker to be turned ON at the same time. Always purchase the official, UL-listed steel interlock bracket manufactured specifically by your panel maker (Square D, Siemens, Eaton, GE) or a third-party manufacturer with an active UL 67 certification report."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Generator Interlock vs Transfer Switch"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Installing the Backfeed Breaker in an Incorrect Panel Slot",
        costImpact: "$450 (Failed Inspection & Interlock Re-drilling)",
        description: "Interlock plates are engineered for exact spatial tolerances between the main service disconnect and specific upper breaker slots (typically slots 2-4 or slots 1-3).",
        solution: "Strictly consult the template included with the interlock kit. Move existing branch circuits down to lower slots to open up the exact designated backfeed location."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Overloading Generator by Leaving Central AC or Water Heater Breakers ON",
        costImpact: "$1,200 (Tripped Generator Thermal Breaker & Stalled Engine)",
